WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.188
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B one.

2
00:00:05.488 --> 00:00:08.851
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:16.366
Unit ten: Defining vs Non-defining Relative Clauses. Today you will decide when a relative clause needs commas.

4
00:00:16.666 --> 00:00:19.272
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:23.443
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:24.890
Who.

7
00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:27.418
Who means used for people.

8
00:00:28.017 --> 00:00:30.063
The doctor who helped me is kind.

9
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:33.186
Your turn. Say: Who.

10
00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:36.524
Who.

11
00:00:37.283 --> 00:00:38.203
Which.

12
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:41.042
Which means used for things.

13
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.917
The car, which is red, is fast.

14
00:00:44.517 --> 00:00:47.924
Your turn. Say: Which.

15
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:51.652
Which.

16
00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:53.116
That.

17
00:00:54.117 --> 00:00:56.639
That means used in defining clauses only.

18
00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:58.959
The car that I bought is red.

19
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.691
Your turn. Say: That.

20
00:01:04.533 --> 00:01:05.256
That.

21
00:01:06.017 --> 00:01:06.797
Comma.

22
00:01:07.783 --> 00:01:10.410
Comma means the sign , used in writing.

23
00:01:11.017 --> 00:01:12.552
Use a comma here.

24
00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.419
Your turn. Say: Comma.

25
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:19.024
Comma.

26
00:01:19.783 --> 00:01:21.295
Essential information.

27
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:25.699
Essential information means details that identify the noun.

28
00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:28.747
Essential information has no commas.

29
00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.594
Your turn. Say: Essential information.

30
00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:36.992
Essential information.

31
00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.192
Extra information.

32
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:43.309
Extra information means details that can be removed.

33
00:01:43.917 --> 00:01:46.183
Extra information needs commas.

34
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:49.923
Your turn. Say: Extra information.

35
00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:54.174
Extra information.

36
00:01:54.917 --> 00:01:55.860
Defining.

37
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.012
Defining means identifying which one.

38
00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:01.825
A defining clause identifies.

39
00:02:02.417 --> 00:02:04.999
Your turn. Say: Defining.

40
00:02:07.833 --> 00:02:08.811
Defining.

41
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:10.644
Non-defining.

42
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.532
Non-defining means giving extra detail only.

43
00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:17.499
A non-defining clause adds detail.

44
00:02:18.083 --> 00:02:20.991
Your turn. Say: Non-defining.

45
00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:25.113
Non-defining.

46
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.458
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:30.317 --> 00:02:36.487
Visal, compare: 'My sister who lives in Paris is a nurse' and 'My sister, who lives in Paris, is a nurse.'

48
00:02:37.583 --> 00:02:49.175
The first suggests I have more than one sister: the one who lives in Paris. The second suggests I have one sister, and the clause is extra information.

49
00:02:50.283 --> 00:02:51.563
How can I tell?

50
00:02:52.650 --> 00:03:07.275
A defining clause is essential: without it the sentence is unclear. No commas, and that is possible. A non-defining clause is extra: we use commas, and we cannot use that.

51
00:03:08.383 --> 00:03:09.930
Can we leave out the pronoun?

52
00:03:11.017 --> 00:03:26.338
In defining clauses, only when it is the object: The book I bought. Never in non-defining clauses. In speech, a pause shows the commas: My brother, who is a pilot, lives abroad.

53
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.221
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:30.083 --> 00:03:34.196
Grammar focus: Defining and non-defining relative clauses.

55
00:03:34.496 --> 00:03:35.706
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.417 --> 00:03:38.799
The man who called is my uncle (no commas).

57
00:03:39.683 --> 00:03:42.716
My uncle, who is a pilot, lives abroad (commas).

58
00:03:43.617 --> 00:03:45.883
The car that I bought is red (that is possible).

59
00:03:46.833 --> 00:03:56.649
A defining clause is essential: no commas, that is possible. A non-defining clause is extra information: commas, never that, and the pronoun cannot be omitted.

60
00:03:57.383 --> 00:04:01.324
Be careful! Don't say: My mother, that is a nurse, is kind.

61
00:04:03.267 --> 00:04:06.218
Say: My mother, who is a nurse, is kind.

62
00:04:06.518 --> 00:04:08.831
Do not use that in a non-defining clause.

63
00:04:09.633 --> 00:04:12.379
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:14.830
Essential or Extra?.

65
00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:17.164
The doctor who helped me is very kind.

66
00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:20.944
Dr Sok, who helped me, is very kind.

67
00:04:21.933 --> 00:04:23.759
The car that I bought is red.

68
00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:27.463
My car, which is red, is very fast.

69
00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:31.291
Commas show extra information that can be removed.

70
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.471
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:37.317 --> 00:04:40.141
My brother, blank lives in Paris, is a pilot.

72
00:04:40.441 --> 00:04:44.384
A, who. B, that. C, which.

73
00:04:47.933 --> 00:04:49.712
The answer is A: who.

74
00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.641
The book blank I read was long.

75
00:04:52.941 --> 00:04:56.281
A, which. B, who. C, whose.

76
00:04:59.833 --> 00:05:01.566
The answer is A: which.

77
00:05:02.717 --> 00:05:05.065
In a non-defining clause, we use blank.

78
00:05:05.365 --> 00:05:08.971
A, commas. B, no commas. C, that only.

79
00:05:12.533 --> 00:05:14.336
The answer is A: commas.

80
00:05:15.483 --> 00:05:16.984
Which sentence is correct?

81
00:05:17.284 --> 00:05:24.815
A, My mother, that is a nurse, is kind.. B, My mother, who is a nurse, is kind.. C, My mother who is a nurse, is kind..

82
00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:31.562
The answer is B: My mother, who is a nurse, is kind..

83
00:05:32.717 --> 00:05:36.109
According to the reading, what does extra information do?

84
00:05:36.409 --> 00:05:41.247
A, it can be removed. B, it is essential. C, it is not true.

85
00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.403
The answer is A: it can be removed.

86
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.566
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:52.417 --> 00:05:56.541
Write one defining and one non-defining relative clause sentence.

88
00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:06.618
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:06.918 --> 00:06:20.812
The girl who sits next to me is called Sophea. My teacher, who is very patient, explains everything twice. The first clause is defining, and the second is non-defining because it has commas.

90
00:06:21.112 --> 00:06:22.774
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:29.354
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and defining and non-defining relative clauses.

92
00:06:29.654 --> 00:06:33.607
Today's value is precision. Choose your words and numbers with care.

93
00:06:33.907 --> 00:06:36.362
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:06:36.662 --> 00:06:39.082
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

